    {{ Live.form("Note.connect(addr)", [["addr", "203.0.113.7:9601"]]) }}
    {{ Live.form("Note.forget(addr)", [["addr", "203.0.113.7:9601"]]) }}
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ===================== Net ===================== -->
<div class="panel">
  <div class="t">Net — distributed execution (workers, training, persisted models)</div>
  <div class="b">
    <p class="lede">The distribution layer as page widgets: register workers (<code>latte worker</code>
    on any machine), evaluate distributable shapes across them, train a model by FedAvg cycles —
    shards on the workers, consolidation in Latte — and use the persisted model straight from the
    page. Naming a store in <code>Net.train</code> commits exactly ONE final-model event.</p>
    {{ Live.watch("Net.workers()", [], 5) }}
    {{ Live.form("Net.addWorker(addr)", [["addr", "127.0.0.1:9700"]]) }}
    {{ Live.form("Net.rmWorker(addr)", [["addr", "127.0.0.1:9700"]]) }}
    {{ Live.form("Net.eval(expr)", [["expr", "(dmap (fn [x] -> (mul x x)) [ 1 [ 2 [ 3 0 ] ] ])"]]) }}
    {{ Live.form("Net.train(rounds, iters, store)", [["rounds", "3"], ["iters", "300"], ["store", "demo"]]) }}
    {{ Live.form("Net.trainLedger(prefix, rounds, iters, store)", [["prefix", "pt."], ["rounds", "3"], ["iters", "300"], ["store", "shared"]]) }}
    {{ Live.box("Net.model(store)", [["store", "demo"]]) }}
    {{ Live.box("Net.predict(store, x)", [["store", "demo"], ["x", "7"]]) }}
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ===================== Live ===================== -->
<div class="panel">
  <div class="t">Live — client-updating widgets <span class="sig">box(expr, [name, default…])</span></div>
  <div class="b">
    <p class="lede">Every interface on this page is one call to
    <code>Live.box(expr, [name, default, …])</code>: it renders an input per name (seeded with its
    default) and a result span showing <code>expr</code> evaluated against them. The server renders
    the initial value; a shared runtime then refreshes just that result on each keystroke. Its
    sibling <code>Live.view(expr, fields)</code> is identical but renders the result as HTML/SVG —
    that is what makes the charts above redraw live. A third sibling,
    <code>Live.watch(expr, fields, secs)</code>, re-runs itself every few seconds with no user
    action — made for NETWORK state (gossiped ledger events, worker liveness) that changes behind
    the page's back; its field list may be empty:</p>
    {{ Live.watch("Kv.info()", [], 6) }}
    <p class="lede">Boxes compose freely — here a Latte expression feeds straight into a Txt helper:</p>

    <div class="tool">
      <div class="lbl"><code>Live.box</code> composing modules — wrap a Latte result in Txt</div>
      {{ Live.form("Latte.eval(expr)", [["expr", "(sum (range 101))"]]) }}
    {{ Live.box("Txt.cap(Txt.join(Txt.words(Latte.eval(expr)), \"-\"))", ["expr", "(range 5)"]) }}
    </div>

    <p class="lede" style="margin-top:.9rem">A field can also be a <b>dropdown</b> of curated
    choices instead of free text. Write fields as lists — <code>[name, default]</code> for a text
    box, or <code>[name, default, option, option, …]</code> for a select. Here the case and the
    separator are both chosen, not typed:</p>

    <div class="tool">
      <div class="lbl"><code>Live.box</code> with dropdowns — choose a transform and a joiner</div>
      {{ Live.box("Txt.join(Txt.words(case), sep)", [["case", "ONE TWO THREE", "ONE TWO THREE", "one two three"], ["sep", "-", "-", " · ", "_", ""]]) }}
    </div>

    <p class="feat">Performance: keystrokes are debounced and every distinct input-set is cached on
    the client, so the server is asked at most once per value; the server memoizes results (keyed by
    library generation) and reuses parsed expressions, so identical or re-typed values are free and
    a fresh value pays only for evaluation. Stale responses are dropped if a newer change has landed.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<footer>Hosted by Hymn, the Orpheus web server. Each frame is a deterministic function of its
tool outputs — the same page, byte-for-byte, every request; the live widgets layer interactivity
on top without disturbing that.</footer>
